<h2>The question</h2>

<p>The operator asked, on 2026-10-03, whether the world has many more delivery strategies than the few made
configurable that day, and whether a <em>decider</em> could pick between them from live signals instead of one fixed
default. This topic holds the survey and the design; the decision is card x1hhjnb. The full working, both surveys
and the comparison are in <a href="/reports/2026-10-03-delivery-strategy-survey-and-decider.md">the session report</a>.</p>

<h2>Measured on 2026-10-03</h2>

<ul>
  <li>About 312 merges to <code>main</code> in 12 hours (26 an hour). PR CI p50 14 min, p90 16 min. One CI run at a
    time serves about 4 merges an hour.</li>
  <li>Of the last 100 CI runs on <code>main</code>, 85 were cancelled, 11 failed and 3 passed. The CI workflow
    cancels in-progress runs per ref, and that applies to pushes to <code>main</code> too, so each landed PR cancels
    the run for the previous head. Every completed run from 12:00Z to 17:24Z failed.</li>
  <li>PR CI failed about 16% of the time (16 of 97), mixing real failures, flakes and red-main fallout.</li>
  <li>5 of 23 open PRs shared a non-backlog file with another open PR.</li>
  <li>GitHub's native merge queue needs an organization-owned repo; this one is user-owned. The statute
    <code>#pr-flow-rollout-mechanism</code> keeps it off anyway.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the world does</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Test changes together on the current trunk.</strong> bors, Zuul, GitLab merge trains, GitHub's merge
    queue, Uber SubmitQueue, Shopify (batch of 8 at about 400 commits a day), Prow Tide.</li>
  <li><strong>Split a failed batch to find the bad change.</strong> The best batch size falls as failures and
    flakes rise: Ericsson data puts it at 9 with few flakes and 4 with more. Zuul grows its window by one on each
    success and halves it on each failure.</li>
  <li><strong>Run only what a change can affect.</strong> Build-graph selection (Bazel, Nx, Turborepo) and
    predictive selection (Meta, Launchable, Google).</li>
  <li><strong>Handle flakes and a red trunk automatically.</strong> Flake scores and quarantine (Meta, Google,
    Slack, GitHub); revert first with culprit finding (Chromium, Google); about 40% of Google breakages were flakes
    with no culprit.</li>
  <li><strong>Avoid conflicts before they exist.</strong> Rosie-style sharding of large changes, barriers around
    global changes, concurrent-edit warnings (ConE), conflict-minimizing task order (Cassandra). Studies of agent
    PRs in 2026 found 28% to 42% conflict rates.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The decider</h2>

<p>One deterministic rule table per decision point (verify order, overlap, integration check, main-red response,
heavy-slot priority, test scope, dispatch admission, wide changes). Each strategy setting gains an opt-in
<code>auto</code> value. Precedence: invariants, then a per-item operator override, then a fixed value, then the
decider, then the platform default. Safety fields may only be tightened. A field starts in shadow mode, and every
decision is journaled with its signals and rule id.</p>

<h2>Where the two surveys disagreed</h2>

<p>Codex argued against building the batched queue early; the Opus draft wanted to un-gate it on red-main
evidence. The skeptic pass sided with Codex: the merged-tree statute already requires testing against the main a
PR lands on, so the fix is to enforce it with a re-check before merge, and the batched queue stays behind its
saturation tripwire. Codex also set a smaller starting batch size, because agent PRs fail in correlated ways.</p>

